    <heading level="1">Everyday Math</heading>
    <exercise number="154" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Buying by the case Joe can buy his favorite ice tea at a convenience store for $$\text{\$1.99}$$ per bottle. At the grocery store, he can buy a case of $$12$$ bottles for $$\text{\$23.88} .$$</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>ⓐ Use the distributive property to find the cost of $$12$$ bottles bought individually at the convenience store. (Hint: notice that $$\text{\$1.99}$$ is $$\text{\$2} - \text{\$0.01} .$$ )</item>
          <item>ⓑ Is it a bargain to buy the iced tea at the grocery store by the case?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="155" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Multi-pack purchase Adele’s shampoo sells for $$\text{\$3.97}$$ per bottle at the drug store. At the warehouse store, the same shampoo is sold as a $$\text{3-pack}$$ for $$\text{\$10.49} .$$</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>ⓐ Show how you can use the distributive property to find the cost of $$3$$ bottles bought individually at the drug store.</item>
          <item>ⓑ How much would Adele save by buying the $$\text{3-pack}$$ at the warehouse store?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>ⓐ 3(4 − 0.03) = 11.91</item>
          <item>ⓑ \$1.42</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
